<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maha Yazawin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1724</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Maha Yazawin-gyi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maha Yazawin (Great Chronicle) is the first national chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, compiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-kala">U Kala</a> and completed around 1724 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo dynasty</a>. It traces Burmese kingship from legendary Buddhist origins to the early 18th century and became the basis for the later Konbaung chronicles, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a> of 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin.jpg" alt="Maha Yazawin" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maha_Yazawin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the Maha Yazawin, Burmese histories were biographies and brief local chronicles. Work on it began around 1712 to 1720, early in the reign of King Taninganway. Its author, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-kala">U Kala</a>, was a wealthy descendant of court and regional officials on both sides of his family, his mother being of mixed Shan and Burman noble descent, and he apparently had access to court documents going back to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>. He prefaced the work with an apology, since Buddhist scriptures held the writing of history to be inimical to religious development, and argued that studying the past showed the impermanence of all things, political power included.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Kala compiled the chronicle in three versions: the Great Chronicle in twenty-one volumes, the Shorter Chronicle in ten and the Brief Chronicle in one. He drew on court records and more than 70 regional and foreign texts, among them the Thaton, Mon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and Chiang Mai chronicles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>. The first two parts deal with the origin of the universe and the Buddhist kings of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>; the third covers the founding of Threhkittara and Pagan and the Pinya, Sagaing, Ava and Toungoo dynasties down to 1711. He left out the Tagaung Kingdom, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a> later presented as the beginning of the Burmese monarchy.</p><p>Its account of 16th-century Toungoo Burma, also seen by many Europeans, has been found largely factual. The earlier periods are sketchier, often giving only the year, because Kala lacked the full earlier chronicles and did not consult inscriptions. It is also the first text to tell of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a>'s conquest of the Thaton Kingdom in 1057, a story the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-aung-thwin">Michael Aung-Thwin</a> has argued appears in no earlier text or inscription.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many of Kala's sources were destroyed in a fire at Ava some twenty years later, which made the chronicle all the more important. The pre-1712 portions of Yazawin Thit (1798), Maha Yazawin Kyaw (1831) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a> largely reproduce it, though they corrected many Pagan and pre-Pagan dates from inscriptions. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-lieberman">Victor Lieberman</a> wrote that rarely has a national tradition depended so heavily on a single author. The last sections, from 1597 to 1711, have been translated into English by Tun Aung Chain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maha Yazawin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monastic schools gave Burma unusually high rates of male literacy for the period, and court literature, legal compilations and chronicles flourished, including U Kala’s great chronicle, the <b>Maha Yazawin</b>, completed in the early eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
